<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wieliczka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wieliczka is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, just south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, known for its rock-salt mine. Salt was extracted there from the 13th century, and together with nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bochnia">Bochnia</a> the mine was a major source of revenue for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> for centuries. Commercial mining ended in 1996, and the mine, with its underground chapels and carved chambers, has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wieliczka.jpg" alt="Wieliczka" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Magistratwieliczka · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wieliczka_widok_na_Zamek_%C5%BBupny_Sztygar%C3%B3wk%C4%99_Rynek_G%C3%B3rny_w_Wieliczce.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wieliczka lies 13 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in the Lesser Poland Voivodeship, in a valley between two ridges that run from west to east. The Wieliczka Salt Mine, one of the world's oldest operating salt mines, was established on significant salt deposits that are also present at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bochnia">Bochnia</a>. The town's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name was Magnum Sal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first settlers were probably from Celtic tribes and were later driven out by West Slavic, or Lechitic, clans. The importance of the mineral deposits grew after Casimir I the Restorer moved the Polish capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in the 11th century, but the Mongol invasion of the 13th century halted mining. Migrating Germans then settled the area and called it Gross Salz, from which the Polish name Wielka Sól came, later becoming Wieliczka. After the discovery in 1252 of large salt and potassium deposits across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, salt was extracted on a much wider scale. In 1290 Duke Premislaus II, later king, granted the town privileges.</p><p>A plague decimated the population in 1651, and during the Swedish Deluge of 1655 to 1660 the Swedes plundered and burned the mine and raised taxes. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 the town passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, and many German, Hungarian, Croatian and Transylvanian miners settled there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Fryderyk Chopin</a> visited in 1828, and in 1846, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> uprising began, rebellious miners seized the salt mines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>German forces entered Wieliczka on 7 September 1939. A ghetto held as many as 11,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> in 1942. In August 1942 all the Jewish population was rounded up: about 700 were shot in a nearby forest, some 700 young men were taken to forced labour camps, and the rest were sent to the killing camp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belzec-extermination-camp">Belzec</a>. Very few survived. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> liberated the town on 21 January 1945. The Wieliczka Salt Mine was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1978, and the old town core was entered in the Register of Historic Monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wieliczka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mongols devastated the duchy in 1241, but under Boleslaw V the Chaste Krakow received town rights in 1257 and the salt mines of <b>Wieliczka</b> and Bochnia brought wealth.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krakow">Duchy of Krakow</a></li></ul></section>
